  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Choose one next planning step using the local decision below. Return exactly one listed statement and stop. No other planning choices have been supplied.\n{\"context\": \"Advance estimates help preparation but do not guarantee acceptance; the same published condition and category guidelines apply to all items at intake.\", \"decision\": \"How should organizers learn what neighbors expect to contribute?\", \"goal\": \"Help usable household goods reach interested neighbors while keeping contributions, claims, and final collection understandable.\", \"problem\": \"Neighbors are organizing a one-day fair for clean, usable household goods that people no longer need. Giving and taking are both optional, and no payment or matching contribution is required. Volunteers have intake space, category tables, reserve shelves, a holding area, carts, and simple item records. Contributors choose in advance whether unclaimed goods should be returned during the closing collection period or donated through an arranged pickup.\"}\nCANDIDATE STATEMENTS:\nOrganizers announce the categories needing more contributions, and neighbors reply with household goods they expect to bring. Advance information begins with gaps in the planned selection, helping contributors identify which of their spare items would be useful.\nBlock volunteers ask interested neighbors about expected contributions and send category summaries to the organizers. A brief local conversation gathers estimates from people who might overlook an online form and combines them for preparation.\nContributors list expected household goods on a shared form before the fair, and organizers total the entries by category. Table preparation follows volunteered estimates while leaving final acceptance to the ordinary intake check."
    }
  ],
